==English==

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|complex|ification}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʃən|s=6}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act or [[process]] of making something more [[complex]].
# {{lb|en|countable|mathematics}} An extension from a basis on [[real number]]s to a basis on [[complex number]]s.

====Quotations====
* {{quote-book|en|title=Liberty and democracy: and other essays in war-time|page=161|author=Hartley Burr Alexander|year=1918|passage=All this represents a change in American society, and a '''complexification''' of it.|indent2=*:}}
* {{quote-text|en|year=1955|author=Teilhard de Chardin|title=The Phenomenon of Man|page=48
|passage=This fundamental discovery that all bodies owe their origin to arrangements of single initial corpuscular type is the beacon that lights the history of the universe to our eyes. In its own way, matter obeyed from the beginning that great law of biology to which we shall have to recur time and time again, the law of ''''complexification''''.}}
* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1981|author=William Irwin Thompson|authorlink=William Irwin Thompson|title=The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture|publisher=Rider/Hutchinson & Co.|location=London|page=154|passage=In Elise Boulding's analysis, the conservative qualities of woman's mysteries chose not to keep pace with the '''complexification''' of urban life.}}
* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=Nicholas Rescher|title=Complexity: A Philosophical Overview|page=56
|passage=For rational beings will of course try simple things first and thereafter be driven step by step towards an ever enhanced '''complexification'''}}
* '''2002''', Charles J. Kibert, Jan Sendzimir{{,}} and G. Bradley Guy (editors), Timothy F.<!--no space-->H. Allen, ''Construction Ecology'', ''Nature as the basis for green buildings'', page 114, Taylor & Francis, {{ISBN|0415260922}}, page 114
*: A new '''complexification''' occurs when the situation becomes desperate through crashing marginal returns on increasing complicatedness.
* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|month=April|author=Andrew D. M. Smith|chapter=Semantic reconstructibility and the complexification of language|title=The Evolution of Language: Proceedings of the 6th International Conference (EVOLANG6)|page=307|publisher=World Scientific|isbn=9812566562
|passage=The development of protolanguage into modern human language, and the '''complexification''' of language more generally, can only occur when language users can successfully communicate even while they maintain different internal representations of language.}}

